1:["$","$Ld",null,{"page":{"id":"phase-2--2c-solidify-your-reading","kind":"phase-article","route":"/roadmap/phase-2/2c-solidify-your-reading","sourceUrl":"https://refold.la/roadmap/phase-2/2c-solidify-your-reading","sourceHash":"769f52e5bd53846f80ecfefbcf4604deef6f6281213d7d08e04ca907f21c7d62","collectedAt":"2026-09-01T23:11:45.385Z","bodyMode":"authorized-full-body","title":{"en":"2C — Solidify your Reading","zh":"2C——巩固你的阅读能力"},"description":{"en":"","zh":""},"segments":[{"id":"what-youre-doing","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"What You're Doing","zh":"你正在做什么"}},{"id":"segment-0002","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"You've built a strong vocabulary and can follow native content with subtitles and a little help. Now it's time to stop relying on those tools.","zh":"你已经建立了扎实的词汇基础，在字幕和少量帮助下能够跟上原生内容。现在是时候停止依赖这些工具了。"}},{"id":"segment-0003","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The goal of this sub-phase is to transition from understanding <em>with</em> tools to understanding <em>without</em> them. You'll continue sentence mining and learning new words, but the emphasis shifts toward freeflow immersion — reading and listening without pausing, without looking things up, and without any safety net. This is where your comprehension becomes real.","zh":"这一子阶段的目标，是从<em>借助</em>工具理解，过渡到<em>不借助</em>工具理解。你会继续进行句子挖掘并学习新词，但重点会转向自由流沉浸——不暂停、不查词，也不依靠任何安全网进行阅读和听力。这是你的理解能力真正发挥作用的阶段。"}},{"id":"segment-0004","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"By the end of this sub-phase, you should be able to sit down with a TV show and enjoy it. Not studying it. Enjoying it.","zh":"在这一子阶段结束时，你应该能够坐下来观看一部电视剧并享受其中。不是学习它，而是享受它。"}},{"id":"whats-new","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"What's New","zh":"有什么新内容"}},{"id":"segment-0006","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"There are no new techniques in this sub-phase. Instead, you're changing the <em>balance</em> of your existing activities:","zh":"这一子阶段没有新的技巧。相反，你正在调整现有活动的<em>平衡</em>："}},{"id":"segment-0007","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>More freeflow, less interactive.</strong> Your freeflow time increases to match your interactive time. This is a deliberate shift. You've spent hundreds of hours building knowledge through interactive immersion — pausing, looking things up, actively working to decode. Now you need to train your brain to use that knowledge at natural speed. <a href=\"/roadmap/library/freeflow-immersion\">Freeflow Immersion</a>","zh":"<strong>增加自由流，减少互动。</strong> 你的自由流时间会增加到与互动时间相当。这是一次有意的调整。你已经通过互动沉浸投入了数百小时来积累知识——暂停、查词、主动努力解码。现在，你需要训练大脑以自然速度运用这些知识。 <a href=\"/roadmap/library/freeflow-immersion\">自由流沉浸</a>"}},{"id":"segment-0008","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Gradually remove your supports.</strong> Throughout Phase 2, you've been using comprehension aids: dictionaries, pausing, replaying, pre-reading summaries, familiar content. Start dropping them one by one. Watch without pausing. Stop looking up every word. Try an episode of something without having seen it before. Each support you remove forces your brain to work harder — and that's exactly what makes you faster. <a href=\"/roadmap/library/comprehensibility-factors\">Comprehensibility Factors</a>","zh":"<strong>逐步移除辅助工具。</strong> 在整个第 2 阶段，你一直在使用理解辅助工具：词典、暂停、回放、预先阅读摘要以及熟悉的内容。现在开始逐一放弃它们。观看时不再暂停。不要再查每一个词。尝试观看一集此前没看过的内容。每移除一种辅助工具，你的大脑就必须更加努力地工作——而这正是让你变快的原因。 <a href=\"/roadmap/library/comprehensibility-factors\">可理解性因素</a>"}},{"id":"segment-0009","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Increase your daily time.</strong> If you've been doing 90 minutes a day, this is a good time to push toward 100-120 minutes. The more freeflow immersion you do, the faster your brain adapts to processing the language at full speed. This is also the point where immersion starts to genuinely feel like entertainment rather than study. <a href=\"/roadmap/phase-0/0d-enjoy-the-process\">0D — Enjoy the Process</a>","zh":"<strong>增加每天的学习时间。</strong> 如果你一直每天学习 90 分钟，现在是逐步增加到 100–120 分钟的好时机。自由流沉浸做得越多，你的大脑就越快适应以全速处理这门语言。这也是沉浸开始真正像娱乐而非学习的阶段。 <a href=\"/roadmap/phase-0/0d-enjoy-the-process\">0D——享受过程</a>"}},{"id":"segment-0010","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Consider the monolingual transition (optional).</strong> If your target language is closely related to your native language, you <em>may</em> want to start shifting your dictionary lookups from bilingual (target → native) to monolingual (target → target). This forces your brain to think in the target language rather than translating. For distant languages, wait until later. <a href=\"/roadmap/library/monolingual-transition\">Monolingual Transition</a>","zh":"<strong>考虑过渡到单语词典（可选）。</strong> 如果你的目标语言与母语关系密切，<em>可以</em>开始将查词方式从双语词典（目标语言 → 母语）逐步转向单语词典（目标语言 → 目标语言）。这会迫使你的大脑用目标语言思考，而不是进行翻译。对于距离较远的语言，可以等到之后再进行。 <a href=\"/roadmap/library/monolingual-transition\">单语过渡</a>"}},{"id":"your-routine","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Your Routine","zh":"你的日常安排"}},{"id":"segment-0012","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The balance continues shifting toward freeflow: <a href=\"/roadmap/library/the-pillars-of-language-learning\">The Pillars of Language Learning</a>","zh":"时间分配继续向自由流沉浸倾斜： <a href=\"/roadmap/library/the-pillars-of-language-learning\">语言学习的支柱</a>"}},{"id":"segment-0013","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"<strong>Priming:</strong> 20%","zh":"<strong>预热：</strong> 20%"},{"en":"<strong>Interactive Immersion:</strong> 40%","zh":"<strong>互动沉浸：</strong> 40%"},{"en":"<strong>Freeflow Immersion:</strong> 40%","zh":"<strong>自由流沉浸：</strong> 40%"}]},{"id":"segment-0014","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Interactive immersion is still important — you're still sentence mining and actively learning new words — but freeflow is now pulling even.","zh":"互动沉浸仍然很重要——你仍在进行句子挖掘并主动学习新词——但自由流沉浸现在正在迎头赶上。"}},{"id":"sample-daily-schedule","type":"heading","level":3,"content":{"en":"Sample Daily Schedule","zh":"每日安排示例"}},{"id":"segment-0016","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"Vocab study (Anki reviews + mined cards): 20 mins","zh":"词汇学习（Anki 复习 + 挖掘出的卡片）：20 分钟"},{"en":"Sentence Mining: 40 mins","zh":"句子挖掘：40 分钟"},{"en":"Freeflow Reading with Audio: 40 mins","zh":"带音频的自由流阅读：40 分钟"}]},{"id":"when-to-move-on","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"When to Move On","zh":"何时进入下一阶段"}},{"id":"segment-0018","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Move to Phase 3 when you:","zh":"满足以下条件时，进入第 3 阶段："}},{"id":"segment-0019","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"Know around 4000 words","zh":"大约掌握 4000 个词"},{"en":"Can enjoy and follow <strong>most</strong> of an adult TV show using only subtitles — no dictionary, no pausing, no tools","zh":"只使用字幕——不查词、不暂停、不使用工具——就能享受并跟上成人电视剧<strong>的大部分内容</strong>"}]},{"id":"segment-0020","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Here's a simple test: pick a new TV show you haven't seen before. Watch two episodes to get used to the characters and setting. If by the third episode, you can follow the plot and understand roughly 90-95% of the words from the subtitles alone, you're ready for Phase 3.","zh":"这里有一个简单的测试：选择一部你以前没看过的新电视剧。先看两集，以熟悉角色和背景。如果到第三集时，只凭字幕就能跟上剧情，并大致理解 90–95% 的词，那么你就可以进入第 3 阶段了。"}},{"id":"research-and-reasoning","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Research and Reasoning","zh":"研究与依据"}},{"id":"segment-0022","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The shift toward freeflow immersion is grounded in skill acquisition theory. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203628942\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DeKeyser (2007, 2015)</a> documented how language abilities move from controlled (conscious, effortful) processing to automatic (unconscious, fluid) processing through sustained practice. Reading with tools is controlled processing — you're consciously working to decode. Freeflow immersion is where that knowledge begins to automatize, and the more you do it, the faster your brain processes language at natural speed.","zh":"转向自由流沉浸的做法有技能习得理论作为基础。<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203628942\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DeKeyser（2007、2015）</a>记录了语言能力如何通过持续练习，从受控处理（有意识、需要付出努力）转变为自动处理（无意识、流畅）。借助工具阅读属于受控处理——你在有意识地努力解码。自由流沉浸则是这些知识开始自动化的阶段；你做得越多，大脑就越快能以自然速度处理语言。"}},{"id":"segment-0023","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The concept of gradually removing supports aligns with research on extensive reading. <a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/9780521568296\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Day &amp; Bamford (1998)</a> made the foundational case that reading large volumes of material at or slightly above your current level — without stopping to look up every word — develops both fluency and a positive attitude toward reading. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1002/tesq.157\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nakanishi (2015)</a> confirmed these benefits in a meta-analysis of 34 studies, finding consistent positive effects on reading proficiency. The underlying principle is that your brain learns to infer meaning from context, and that inferencing ability is crucial for real-world comprehension. Our adaptation is to focus on reading from TV shows and subtitles, rather than books (which were the focus of many studies prior to the widespread use of and access to foreign media from anywhere in the world).","zh":"逐步移除辅助的理念与泛读研究相一致。<a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/9780521568296\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Day &amp; Bamford（1998）</a>奠定了基础：大量阅读处于当前水平或略高于当前水平的材料——无需停下来查每一个词——既能培养流利度，也能提升对阅读的积极态度。<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1002/tesq.157\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nakanishi（2015）</a>对34项研究进行元分析，证实了这些益处，并发现泛读对阅读能力有持续的积极影响。其基本原理是：大脑会学着从语境中推断意义，而这种推断能力对于现实生活中的理解至关重要。我们的调整是专注于阅读电视节目和字幕，而不是书籍（许多早期研究都以书籍为重点，因为当时还没有如今这样广泛地接触世界各地外语媒体的机会）。"}}],"cards":[]},"siteBasePath":"/my_learning_docs"}]
